Apparently 28 is the age when you stop caring about Instagramming a midnight kiss.
This year, I'll kiss my husband. We'll have a precious little countdown, we'll bang some pans together instead of buying 10-cent noisemakers, and we'll watch the ball drop on TV — with my parents.
Yes, I'm 28, and I'm excited at the prospect of staying in, playing dominoes and kicking it with a pair of Baby Boomers.
I should caveat that my mom and stepdad know how to rage, which closes the perceived lameness gap. When visiting us in New York City, they tempt my friends and I with promises of dinner and then we wake up the next morning wondering why our hair hurts. Their house in California is the center of family gatherings (we had 23 people over for Christmas), and they have a closet full of board games and a dope wine collection.
Looks like we made it! #2013 A photo posted by Stephanie Buck (@stephmbuck) on Jan 1, 2013 at 12:22am PST
So, it's not like I'll be ringing in 2016 at a retirement home, but it's the first time I can remember gladly cooping up in the house where I grew up. (The other memory was 2000 with my dad and 400 canned vegetables in a semi-panicked attempt to ward off the Y2K global system meltdown and subsequent zombie apocalypse.)
But I will admit that a night of mellow reflection at home over vomit hopscotch on the streets of New York sounds amazing this time.
It's nothing new that more millennials are acting like grandparents. We've been dubbed "Generation Yawn" not only for our nostalgic penchant for crafts and shuffleboard, but also because we're worn down.
The information age has never moved faster, and millennials are struggling to strike a balance among work, hobbies, friendships and, for some, parenting. More millennials than Boomers find it difficult to manage work and family/personal responsibilities (35 percent vs. 30 percent, respectively); 47 percent of millennials cite increased responsibilities at work as a leading cause, and 44 percent cite more responsibilities at home, according to a 2014 Ernst & Young Global Generations Research survey.
2015 is weird so far and I'm into it A photo posted by Stephanie Buck (@stephmbuck) on Dec 31, 2014 at 9:09pm PST
In addition to feeling these stresses myself, the latter part of 2015 was one of my most challenging yet. My husband and I packed up our belongings in New York and hit the road for California, where we both grew up. It meant leaving dozens of friends we've grown to love, offices we've grown accustomed to (though, I will still work at Mashable in San Francisco. Thanks, bosses!), and a city that's a year-round, nonstop party — whether you're paying $800 for bottle service or sitting in Central Park with a sandwich.
Gogol Bordello just made my top 10 concerts. Happy New Year, gypsies!! #nye A photo posted by Stephanie Buck (@stephmbuck) on Jan 1, 2014 at 9:06am PST
After weeks of looking for apartments, saying goodbyes and planning our road trip across the country (yes, it took six days; no, I'm not ready to talk about it), we arrived at home to three consecutive days of holiday celebrations, more apartment hunting and a promise to return to work the Monday following Christmas.
It's Wednesday now. I've survived — with a lot of meditation, cry therapy and hugs. Many hugs.
What's interesting is that, at age 28, I'm not alone. Even though I made the move and crammed in a few 18-hour days of family arm-tugging (did I mention I'm an introvert?), my friends have admitted they are equally exhausted. More of them than ever are relishing the opportunity to stay home on major FOMO holidays — New Year's, Halloween and the dreaded Santacon.
We had the giggs. Photo by @chrisphillips25. #latergram #NYE #lalaladyday A photo posted by Stephanie Buck (@stephmbuck) on Jan 2, 2015 at 6:12pm PST
Partly it's because I have a concentrated group of friends in major cities — where New Year's tickets plus Uber surge pricing can cost upwards of $300 per person, where high heels just aren't worth the pain anymore, when you've barely recovered from a 55-hour workweek, plus days of parents pleading for grandchildren.
Welcome to your late twenties.
