Dana Point wanted the biggest fine possible slapped on a medical-marijuana dispensary that settled in town about two years ago. A judge gave the city what it wanted.
Orange County Superior Court Judge William Monroe signed the city��s proposed order Wednesday, telling Beach Cities Collective and its owner, David Lambert, to pay about $2.44 million in civil damages, the city announced Thursday. That��s the maximum allowed for violations of health and safety and business and professions codes. In addition, the dispensary cannot reopen at 26841 Calle Hermosa.
Jeffrey Schwartz, Beach Cities�� attorney, said the dispensary plans to appeal the decision.
��It��s ridiculously high,�� Schwartz said. ��Those figures are set as a maximum under law. If somebody is out and truly creating a nuisance like traffic jams and brawls in the street, that��s where the maximum fines come in.
��In this case, there have been no arrests. There hasn��t even been a ticket for loitering or even littering.��
Marijuana advocates said they were surprised by the judgment.
��I��ve never heard of one like that,�� said Dale Gieringer, spokesman for California NORML, a marijuana advocacy group. ��There are hundreds of lawsuits, and most keep going on. They don��t reach the summary judgment level.��
The summary judgment, made without a trial, came after the city this month won a lawsuit that accused the pot shop of operating illegally and being a public nuisance.
The city has been fighting several dispensaries in town for almost two years, claiming they were operating illegally. The battle has taken on twists and turns throughout the court system, with some skirmishes being fought at the Superior Court, appellate and state Supreme Court levels.
At one point, Dana Point had six marijuana dispensaries. Some pulled out, and the city shut down Beach Cities, Holistic Health and The Point Alternative Care in January, citing municipal code violations such as unsafe entrances and building additions.
