The winds of change were clearly in the air Friday at Hanson Stadium.
For one of the few times this spring, temperatures climbed above the 60-degree mark, just in time for the rejunvenated Lane Tech boys track team to blow defending City Relays champion Steinmetz from its lofty pedestal and claim the title.
Lane registered 82 points in the two-day event to easily outdistance the 30-team field. Hirsch finished second with 55, followed by King (49), Young (39) and Chicago Vocational (34). Hubbard (32), Du Sable (28), Austin (24), Julian (23) and Lake View (20) rounded out the top 10.
Steinmetz, meanwhile, finished a distant 14th with just 14 points. During Tuesday's preliminaries, Eagles coach Ed Wallace pulled his team out of all the relay events because of the cold weather, and on Friday only fielded competitors in the field events.
"I've been coaching for more than 30 years now," said Wallace, "and my experience has been that when you compete in conditions like Tuesday, you usually suffer big-time injuries. I just didn't think it was worth it.
"Our kids were disappointed, but I think they understand that it's important for us to be healthy for the city finals next month. And judging by what I saw out there (Friday), that's going to be a pretty tough field. Everybody looked good."
