The PC version of DICE��s shooter is the most technically advanced, but what difference do the better graphics and extra players really make?
There is no irony in the fact that, as all eyes are fixed on the next generation versions, Battlefield 4 looks and plays best on a PC. Nor is there any surprise, since developer DICE has warned as much for months. The series not only began on the PC but thrived there for years, completely unknown to most console gamers. Times have changed now of course but at heart DICE are still PC developers first and foremost, and on the PC is still the best way to play Battlefield.
To be fair though the differences between the PC version at a medium to high setting and the PlayStation 4 are very mild (we still haven��t played the Xbox One version and are in any case not allowed to review any Xbox One games until later in the month). But it��s not really the improved graphics which make such a difference to how Battlefield 4 plays, but rather the increase in the maximum number of online players from 24 to 64.
As popular as Battlefield 3 has been on the current gen consoles it was really only a shadow of the PC version, and the technical improvements in this sequel have only make porting the game down to the Xbox 360 even more difficult – as discussed in our review. Where there is rough parity between all the versions though is the single-player mode, which is rubbish no matter what format you play it on.
Although Battlefield 4��s story campaign isn��t as worthless as its equivalent in Battlefield 3 it��s still an unsatisfying halfway house between Call Of Duty style bluster and the real Battlefield multiplayer experience. There are some great cinematic set pieces, but the story is so full of cliché – from the dialogue and characters to the now de rigueur torture scene – that you get the increasing impression it��s only there to provide footage for the trailers.
It is certainly less on-the-rails than Battlefield 3, but having any kind or restriction on where you can drive or fly vehicles runs completely counter to the spirit of Battlefield. That��s one of the core problems with any story-based content in Battlefield; that and the still appalling artificial intelligence, which sees allies wander around like distracted housewives at a shopping centre and bad guys that seem more desperate than you to have a bullet hole put between their eyes.
But that the single-player is so forgettable doesn��t really matter. It would��ve been nice if it worked as a training tool for the multiplayer but since DICE don��t seem interested in putting it to that use it��s best to think of it more as an optional extra. Online multiplayer is what Battlefield 4 is all about and there��s still no other game that can match its huge maps, dozens of different vehicles, and convincing veneer of realism. Well, no other game except Battlefield 3 anyway.
Even on the PC at first, second, and often third glance Battlefield 4 looks almost identical to its predecessor. It depends what you��re doing at the time of course, but in terms of the second-to-second gameplay this feels more like an expansion than a true sequel. That��s no more of a criticism that it would be for any other great multiplayer games though -from Call Of Duty to Mario Kart – and there are some notable improvements to the formula, even if none of them make quite the sort of difference DICE would like to pretend.
The headliner is probably the appallingly named Levolution, which is not a French toilet water but a new style of destruction effect that ranges from an exploding petrol station to a bursting dam that floods a map with water and completely changes the nature of the battle. Well, that one does. The others, like the famous collapsing skyscraper, make relatively little difference in practice and there��s no guarantee that it��ll happen while you��re playing or that you��ll even see it if it does.
The other big new feature, or rather big new old feature, is Commander Mode, which used to be a key part of the series prior to Battlefield 3. It allows anyone to play the game via a real-time strategy style view of the battlefield. From here you can set objectives, send supplies, organise artillery support and vehicle drops, or highlight enemy positions.
You have to be at least level 10 to use it but it works very well, especially if you download the tablet app to control it. There��s no guarantee anyone will take any notice of you though, and again the actual difference it makes in battle is often open to question. A good commander gives a small but definite advantage to his team but a bad, or even mediocre, one often becomes a liability (handily you can fire your boss if they��re not working out).
Beyond this the game��s list of new features begins to read more like an expansion pack than a full sequel, with two new game modes called Obliteration and Defuse. Obliteration has two teams fighting over a randomly spawned bomb, which you have to get over to the enemy��s base and detonate. The Counter-Strike inspired Defuse has you trying to attack or defend a bomb in a series of rounds that last only a few minutes and have no vehicles or redeployment.
They��re fun modes but the game doesn��t even try to pretend that they��re anything other than side dishes, with Conquest remaining Battlefield��s main course – as you fight over control points in the traditional manner.
