Nursing-home industry lobbyists cheered Friday night as the Legislature approved a comprehensive legislative package aimed at ending headline-grabbing jury awards against nursing homes.
The nursing home reform bill (CS-SB1202), which includes a $4 million cap on punitive damages in most cases and a dramatic increase in staffing requirements, passed the Senate on a 38-0 vote and cleared the House 109-8.
The bill now goes to the desk of Gov. Jeb Bush. He is expected to sign the bill into law, even though two years ago he said the lawsuit limits contained in the bill could do harm to vulnerable citizens, such as nursing-home residents.
��We have something here which is going to substantially improve the quality of care for all our elders,�� said Sen. Ginny Brown-Waite, R-Spring Hill, the chief architect of the bill.
Critics of the bill said the measure has many shortcomings, arguing that new staffing guidelines will only bring very modest increases in the professional attention given to nursing-home residents.
This is the third year in a row that lawmakers have tried to stem the flow of lawsuits against nursing homes and mandate higher standards of care. The fight has pitted nursing-home operators, who complain that lawsuits are driving them into financial ruin, against trial attorneys, who argue that they wouldn't sue if nursing homes took better care of their residents.
A task force appointed by the governor last year failed to come up with a set of recommendations, and a heavily financed media campaign saturated the airwaves with commercials urging the elderly and their families to contact lawmakers.
The bill has taken several twists throughout the session, with major changes made even in the last few hours of debate that affected the funding for the bill. Earlier in the week, lawmakers said the measure devoted $60 million to nursing-home care, but the final package spends nearly $77 million to meet a promise by Senate President John McKay to make nursing-home quality the No. 1 priority of the Legislature.
The bill makes it more difficult for lawyers to sue a nursing home, raises the standards for collecting damages and ends the practice of awarding attorney fees in cases that don't involve death or serious injury.
In exchange for the lawsuit limits, the state steers nearly $77 million to nursing homes to raise their nursing care gradually over the next few years.
